WEBVTT


00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:04.960
A funny thing sometimes happens
when you rehang stitches.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:11.500
Anytime you rehang a cast on edge,
you end up with one less stitch.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.030
Have you ever noticed that?

00:00:14.129 --> 00:00:16.379
I'm knitting an ear flap for a hat.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.830
I've worked in open cast
on with scrap and ravel.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.400
Let's say I cast on 20 stitches.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.760
I knit my triangle and the ties.

00:00:26.955 --> 00:00:31.905
Now when it's time to rehang the flap on
the needle bed to join that flap to the

00:00:31.905 --> 00:00:35.955
hat body, I can only find
19 stitches to pick up.

00:00:36.705 --> 00:00:37.455
Why is that?

00:00:38.075 --> 00:00:41.975
In this drawing, I've cast on
and knit a bit over six needles.

00:00:42.365 --> 00:00:44.644
Six columns of stitches, right?

00:00:45.935 --> 00:00:52.295
When I rotate the knitting to pick up a
hem or hang my triangle flaps, it looks

00:00:52.295 --> 00:00:55.535
like there are only five
full stitches to pick up.

00:00:56.114 --> 00:00:57.765
It's just the nature of knitting.

00:00:58.245 --> 00:01:00.765
We have a half stitch on each end.

00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:03.295
What to do?

00:01:03.925 --> 00:01:05.755
Well, it depends on what you're knitting.

00:01:06.595 --> 00:01:07.445
For a hem.

00:01:07.495 --> 00:01:09.565
This probably isn't going to matter.

00:01:10.685 --> 00:01:15.905
But you could pick up one of those
half stitches to keep your stitch count

00:01:15.905 --> 00:01:16.625
accurate.

00:01:18.095 --> 00:01:22.925
You could use the tail of yarn that's
close by and eWrap an extra stitch.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.190
The solution is really up to you, but this
is just something to be aware of and keep

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:34.080
in mind to keep your stitch counts
accurate when you're picking up stitches.

